# Seasonal Idle ARPG  
## Technical Architecture Specification v1.0

## 1. Document Purpose

This document defines the technical architecture for the browser version of Seasonal Idle ARPG. It is intended to guide frontend, backend, UI, and systems implementation for the first playable version and future seasonal expansion.

The architecture prioritizes:
- Fast browser load time.
- Clear separation between combat and management UI.
- Deterministic, data-driven combat.
- Seasonal reset support.
- Easy content expansion.

## 2. Architecture Goals

### 2.1 Product Goals
- Support short, repeatable play sessions.
- Enable automated combat and idle progression.
- Make seasonal resets safe and easy to operate.
- Keep the client lightweight and browser-friendly.

### 2.2 Engineering Goals
- Use a maintainable, typed codebase.
- Separate game logic from presentation.
- Make combat deterministic and testable.
- Support rapid iteration on content and balance.
- Keep the architecture simple enough for a small team.

### 2.3 Non-Goals
- Real-time multiplayer combat.
- Open-world streaming.
- 3D rendering.
- Heavy physics simulation.
- Complex authoritative action networking.

## 3. Recommended Stack

### 3.1 Frontend
- **Language:** TypeScript
- **Game engine:** Phaser 3
- **App bundler:** Vite
- **UI framework:** React for non-combat menus, or a DOM-based UI layer if you want lower integration complexity
- **State layer:** Zustand or a lightweight event/store system
- **Audio:** Web Audio API or Phaser audio system

Phaser is well suited to desktop and mobile HTML5 games, and the Phaser + Vite template is an official starting point for this kind of workflow. [phaser](https://phaser.io/news/2024/01/phaser-vite-template)
React is recommended only for app-like screens such as inventory, skill setup, season progress, settings, and mission boards, while Phaser owns battle scenes and rendering-heavy gameplay. [gamineai](https://gamineai.com/blog/game-development-with-typescript-building-browser-games-modern-javascript)

### 3.2 Backend
- **Runtime:** Node.js
- **API style:** REST for core game operations
- **Realtime:** WebSocket only if needed later for leaderboards, live events, or synchronized seasonal content
- **Database:** PostgreSQL
- **Cache / queue:** Redis optional, recommended once scale or seasonal jobs grow

### 3.3 DevOps
- **Source control:** Git
- **CI:** GitHub Actions or similar
- **Hosting:** Static frontend CDN + backend service host + managed PostgreSQL
- **Observability:** application logs, error tracking, basic analytics

